      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the measure of \\(\\angle CED\\). Let's proceed step by step.\n\n1. **Identify Key Angles and Properties:**\n   - \\(AB\\) is a diameter of the circle, so \\(\\angle AOB = 180^\\circ\\).\n   - \\(E\\) is a point on the circle, so \\(\\angle AEB = 180^\\circ - \\angle BAE = 180^\\circ - 43^\\circ = 137^\\circ\\).\n   - \\(O\\) is the center of the circle, and the tangents at \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) are perpendicular to the radii \\(OB\\) and \\(OE\\), respectively.\n   - Therefore, \\(\\angle OBE = 90^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle OEB = 90^\\circ\\).\n\n2. **Determine \\(\\angle OBE\\) and \\(\\angle OEB\\):**\n   - Since \\(\\angle OBE = 90^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle OEB = 90^\\circ\\), and \\(\\angle AEB = 137^\\circ\\), we need to find \\(\\angle OEC\\).\n   - Note that \\(\\angle OEC = 180^\\circ - \\angle OEB = 180^\\circ - 90^\\circ = 90^\\circ\\) because \\(OEC\\) is a straight line through \\(E\\).\n\n3. **Calculate \\(\\angle CED\\):**\n   - Now, consider \\(\\triangle COD\\). Since \\(OC\\) and \\(OD\\) are radii of the circle, \\(\\angle ODC = 90^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle OCD = 90^\\circ\\).\n   - We need to find \\(\\angle CED\\). Note that \\(\\angle CED = \\angle OEC - \\angle OED\\).\n   - Since \\(\\angle OEC = 90^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle OED = \\angle ODE - \\angle ODE = \\angle ODE + \\angle DOE\\) (because \\(\\angle ODE = \\angle OED\\) and they are equal), and \\(\\angle OED = 90^\\circ\\) (because \\(OED\\) is a right angle at \\(D\\)), we have:\n     \\[\n     \\angle CED = \\angle OEC - \\angle OED = 90^\\circ - (90^\\circ - \\angle BAE) = \\angle BAE = 43^\\circ.\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the measure of \\(\\angle CED\\) is \\(\\boxed{47}\\).",
